>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Dairy science career options in India run through two genuinely different tracks — India’s cooperative dairy federations and its private dairy companies — and the honest picture depends on which one you actually build toward.</strong>
    India is the world’s largest milk producer, but only about a fifth to a quarter of that milk moves
    through the organised sector where dairy-science roles actually exist. Inside that organised sector,
    a B.Tech Dairy Technology graduate from NDRI Karnal or a state agricultural university, or a B.Sc
    Dairy Science graduate, can build a real career in plant operations, product development, milk
    procurement, quality systems, or FSSAI-linked regulatory work — and eventually into senior plant
    leadership or an independent value-added dairy products business, which India’s cooperative dairy
    culture makes a genuinely realistic path, not a rare exception.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, dairy science is a real, specialisable career — but it splits into a cooperative track (Amul/GCMMF, Mother Dairy, Nandini, Verka, Aavin) and a private track (Hatsun, Nestlé, Britannia, Parag, Heritage, Dodla), with different pay and security trade-offs.</li>
      <li>Entry runs through BTech Dairy Technology (NDRI Karnal’s all-India quota is genuinely tiny; agricultural universities hold the larger state-quota pool), BSc Dairy Science, or a faster diploma route.</li>
      <li>Fresher pay runs roughly ₹2.4-5.5 lakh a year depending on employer type; senior plant leadership or R&D specialists reach ₹15-28 lakh over 12-18 years.</li>
      <li>India’s milk production is the world’s largest, but only about 20-25% moves through the organised sector — that share, not the headline number, is what drives real hiring.</li>
      <li><strong>Building a genuine specialisation — QA systems, procurement, or value-added R&D — or eventually an independent dairy-processing business is what turns this degree into real high income opportunities, not the degree alone.</strong></li>
      <li>NDRI admission, a master’s degree, and the ARS exam are each separate, genuinely competitive filters — not automatic next steps from one another.</li>
    </ul>
