  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer on dairy science career options in India</h2>

  <p>
    Dairy science is a real, focused branch built on top of one fact most people already know but rarely
    connect to a career: India makes more milk than any other country on earth. What most guides skip is
    that this scale does not translate directly into jobs — it translates into jobs specifically inside
    the organised cooperative and private dairy-processing sector, which is a smaller, more specific slice
    of the total milk economy.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest split is this: the sector-level tailwind — rising packaged and value-added dairy demand,
    a growing organised-sector share — is genuinely real. The entry-level reality for an individual
    graduate depends heavily on which of the two employment tracks, cooperative or private, they actually
    target, and most college brochures blur that choice into one vague "dairy job" outcome.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "India is the world’s largest milk producer, so join now" pitch that agricultural
      college brochures lean on, and it is not a warning to avoid the field either. Dairy science genuinely
      rewards people who understand the cooperative-versus-private choice and build a real specialisation
      — and genuinely disappoints people who assumed "a dairy job" meant one fixed, simple outcome.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cooperative-model">The two-track employment model most guides skip</h2>

  <p>
    Here is the structural fact that separates dairy science from almost every other agri-food branch:
    the industry runs on two genuinely different employment systems at once, and a graduate’s early-career
    experience depends almost entirely on which one they enter.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
