        {awarenessComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Neither track is automatically "better." The cooperative track — India’s Anand Pattern of village
      society, district union, and state federation — offers structured, PSU-like job security at slower
      pay growth. The private track offers faster pay growth and corporate-style progression at less
      security. Choosing which track to target, and researching the specific union or company rather than
      the brand name, is the single highest-leverage decision a dairy-science student can make before
      graduating.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="entry-routes">NDRI, BTech, and BSc: the real entry routes</h2>

  <p>
    "Dairy technology" and "dairy science" get used almost interchangeably, but the admission routes into
    them genuinely differ, and the difference matters for how competitive your entry actually is.
  </p>

  <div class="track-grid">
    {entryRoutes.map((card) => (
      <div class="track-card">
        <h3>{card.title}</h3>
        <p>{card.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      <strong>This is a two-stage competitive gate, not one.</strong> The first gate is getting into NDRI
      Karnal’s all-India-quota BTech seats specifically — a genuinely tiny cohort nationally, filled
      through a competitive AIEEA-UG rank against a very small number of places. The second gate sits years
      later and is separate from the first: landing one of the limited cooperative-federation or
      private-brand roles, clearing an irregular state PSC cycle for an FSSAI Food Safety Officer post, or
      later clearing the ASRB Agricultural Research Service exam for a Scientist post at NDRI or another
      ICAR institute.
    </p>
    <p>
      Clearing the first gate meaningfully improves your odds at the second — it does not guarantee it. A
      real share of NDRI graduates still compete hard for a small pool of top-tier openings, which is a
      separate, later hurdle from the admission cutoff itself.
    </p>
  </div>

