  </p>

  <p>
    So does dairy science have real growth headroom, or is it structurally capped? The honest answer: it
    is capped early and slow-moving through the generalist middle on both tracks, but it is not capped at
    the top on either one. The ceiling that opens up — senior plant leadership, R&D-lead roles, or
    senior cooperative-federation management — belongs specifically to people who build a documented
    specialisation, not to tenure or the degree alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="career-lanes">The real career lanes in dairy science</h2>

  <p>
    "Dairy science" is not one job. It is five genuinely different work modes, each with its own daily
    reality, hiring volume, and pay ceiling.
  </p>

  <p>
    The order below leads with production and plant operations because it combines the highest hiring
    volume with the clearest documented ladder into senior private-sector pay (a Plant Head or Regional
    Quality-Operations Head reaches roughly ₹15-28 lakh a year). R&D for value-added products comes next
    because, despite fewer openings, it carries the strongest per-role ceiling and the most direct bridge
    toward eventually starting your own dairy-processing business. Procurement sits third specifically
    because it is a role unique to this field with no real equivalent elsewhere. Regulatory sits last
    here because it is capped by slow, structured government pay bands rather than open market growth —
    a real and stable lane, just not the one with the most headroom.
  </p>

