        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--gold">
    <p>
      <strong>A sixth path worth naming plainly, not just folding into "specialise": ownership.</strong>
      Outside all five employed lanes above, a genuinely scalable option is starting your own small
      value-added dairy processing unit (paneer, cheese, ghee, flavoured milk), building an
      FSSAI-compliance consulting practice for smaller dairy producers, or running a franchise-style
      processing-and-parlour setup.
    </p>
    <p>
      It scales in a way a salaried plant or R&D role never does, and India’s cooperative dairy culture
      makes it a genuinely normalised path rather than an exotic one: raw milk supply is hyper-local and
      reliable across most regions, branded demand for packaged paneer, cheese, and ghee is growing fast,
      and a small processor can realistically start with far less capital than most other food-manufacturing
      businesses require.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="market-scale">What India's milk numbers actually mean for hiring</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part of the "world’s largest milk producer" pitch that needs an honest translation before
    it becomes a career signal instead of just a trivia fact.
  </p>

