      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Automation is spreading fast across the routine, scriptable half of dairy-plant work — automated
      milk-quality analysers, CIP monitoring, digital batch logging. It is not touching sensory judgment,
      root-cause investigation, or the human, trust-based work of milk procurement — the exact work that
      still needs a person who can read the situation and take responsibility for the call.
    </p>
    <p>
      The safer response is pairing core dairy-science knowledge with digital quality-systems fluency —
      knowing how to work with, check, and improve an automated plant workflow — rather than avoiding the
      field or ignoring the shift entirely. This is realistic to build on the job over time, in stages, not
      something that requires becoming a software engineer.
    </p>
  </div>

